Using ALMA's H30α (231.90 GHz) observations of the metal-poor dwarf galaxy NGC 5253, we estimate the galaxy's star formation rate (SFR) and assess the reliability of other SFR measurement techniques. Based on these observations, the galaxy's photoionizing photon production rate and SFR (0.087±0.013 M$_\odot$ yr$^{-1}$) were calculated, with the SFR derived from the total infrared flux yielding similar results. SFR values based on previously-corrected Hα flux data were also similar to those from the H30α data, while mid-infrared and combined Hα/mid-infrared SFR values were higher than expected. Conversely, the 70 and 160 μm flux densities resulted in SFR values that were lower than the H30α-based value, providing evidence for contamination of the mid-infrared and 350 μm fluxes by emission from the galaxy's dust grains, with the galactic UV background also affecting the SFR values based on the H30α data. Our results provide guidelines for determining SFR values from the ALMA Fornax Cluster project's data using the following formulae: F$_{
